WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.100
Sometimes it feels like the universe is just&nbsp;
messing with you. I have up on screen here a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.100 --> 00:00:09.180
sequence of computations and don't worry, in&nbsp;
a moment we're going to unpack and visualize&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:09.180 --> 00:00:12.600
what each one is really saying. What I&nbsp;
want you to notice is how the sequence&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:12.600 --> 00:00:18.000
follows a very predictable, if random seeming&nbsp;
pattern, and how each computation happens to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:18.000 --> 00:00:22.860
equal pi. And if you are just messing around&nbsp;
evaluating these on a computer for some reason,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:22.860 --> 00:00:28.020
you might think that this was a pattern that would&nbsp;
go on forever. But it doesn't. At some point,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:28.020 --> 00:00:34.440
it stops and instead of equaling pi, you get a&nbsp;
value which is just barely, barely less than pi.

00:00:39.600 --> 00:00:43.620
Alright, let's dig into what's going on here. The&nbsp;
main character in the story today is the function&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:43.620 --> 00:00:48.060
sin of X divided by X. This actually comes&nbsp;
up commonly enough in math and engineering&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:48.060 --> 00:00:52.140
that it gets its own name, "sinc" and the way&nbsp;
you might think about it is by starting with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:52.140 --> 00:00:56.160
a normal oscillating sin curve and then&nbsp;
sort of squishing it down as you get far&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:56.160 --> 00:01:01.080
away from zero by multiplying it by 1 over&nbsp;
X and the astute among you might ask about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:01.080 --> 00:01:05.880
what happens at X equals 0 since when you plug&nbsp;
that in, it looks like dividing zero by zero&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:05.880 --> 00:01:10.440
and then the even more astute among you may&nbsp;
fresh out of a calculus class could point out&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:10.440 --> 00:01:14.820
that for values closer and closer to zero,&nbsp;
the function gets closer and closer to one.

00:01:14.820 --> 00:01:20.880
So, if we simply redefine the sinc function at&nbsp;
0 to equal 1, you get a nice continuous curve.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:20.880 --> 00:01:25.200
All of that is a little by the by because&nbsp;
the thing we actually care about is the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:25.200 --> 00:01:29.820
integral of this curve from negative infinity&nbsp;
to infinity. Which you think of as meaning the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:29.820 --> 00:01:34.800
area between the curve and the X axis, or&nbsp;
more precisely the signed area. Meaning you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:34.800 --> 00:01:38.700
add all the area bound by the positive&nbsp;
parts of the graph in the X axis and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:38.700 --> 00:01:42.420
you subtract all of the parts found by the&nbsp;
negative parts of the graph and the X axis.

00:01:42.420 --> 00:01:47.100
Like we saw at the start, it happens to be&nbsp;
the case that this evaluates to be exactly pi,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:47.100 --> 00:01:51.420
which is nice and also a little weird&nbsp;
and it's not entirely clear how you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:51.420 --> 00:01:54.780
would approach this with the usual tools&nbsp;
of calculus. Towards the end of the video,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:54.780 --> 00:01:56.580
I'll share the trick for how you would do this.

00:01:56.580 --> 00:02:00.780
Progressing on with the sequence I opened&nbsp;
with, the next step is to take a copy of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:00.780 --> 00:02:06.000
sinc function where you plug in X/3, which&nbsp;
will basically look like the same graph but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:06.000 --> 00:02:11.100
stretched out horizontal by a factor of three.&nbsp;
When we multiply these two functions together,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:11.100 --> 00:02:15.780
we get a much more complicated wave whose&nbsp;
mass seems to be more concentrated towards&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:15.780 --> 00:02:20.040
the middle and with any usual functions, you&nbsp;
would expect this completely changes the area.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:20.040 --> 00:02:23.760
You can't just randomly modify an integral&nbsp;
like this and expect nothing to change.

00:02:23.760 --> 00:02:28.500
So already, it's a little bit weird that&nbsp;
this result also equals pi, that nothing has&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:28.500 --> 00:02:32.700
changed. That's another mystery you should&nbsp;
add to your list. And the next step in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:32.700 --> 00:02:36.600
sequence was to take an even more stretched out&nbsp;
version of this sinc function by a factor of 5,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:36.600 --> 00:02:42.480
multiply that by what we already have and again,&nbsp;
look at the signed area underneath the whole curve,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:42.480 --> 00:02:47.340
which again equals pi. And it continues&nbsp;
on like this; with each iteration,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:47.340 --> 00:02:50.940
we stretch out by a new odd number&nbsp;
and multiply that into what we have.

00:02:50.940 --> 00:02:55.920
One thing you might notice is how except&nbsp;
that the input X equals 0, every single&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:55.920 --> 00:02:59.700
part of this function is progressively getting&nbsp;
multiplied by something that's smaller than one.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:00.300 --> 00:03:04.440
So you would expect as the sequence progresses&nbsp;
for things to get squished down more and more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:04.440 --> 00:03:08.940
and if anything, you would expect the&nbsp;
area to be getting smaller. Eventually,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:08.940 --> 00:03:14.880
that is exactly what happens but what's bizarre is&nbsp;
that it stays so stable for so long and of course,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:14.880 --> 00:03:20.700
more pertinently, that when it does break at the&nbsp;
value 15 it does so by the tiniest tiny amount.

00:03:20.700 --> 00:03:24.300
And before you go thinking this is the&nbsp;
result of some numerical error, maybe&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:24.300 --> 00:03:28.260
because we're doing something with floating point&nbsp;
arithmetic, if you work this out more precisely,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:28.260 --> 00:03:33.900
here is the exact value of that last integral,&nbsp;
which is a certain fraction of pi where the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:33.900 --> 00:03:38.940
numerator and the denominator are absurd.&nbsp;
They're both around 400 billion billion billion.

00:03:40.380 --> 00:03:43.740
So, this pattern was described&nbsp;
in a paper by a father-son pair;&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:43.740 --> 00:03:47.340
Jonathan and David Borwein, which is&nbsp;
very fun, and they mentioned how when&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:47.340 --> 00:03:50.880
a fellow researcher was computing these&nbsp;
integrals using a computer algebra system,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:50.880 --> 00:03:56.400
he assumed that this had to be some kind of bug,&nbsp;
but it's not a bug. It is a real phenomenon.

00:03:56.400 --> 00:04:00.840
And it gets weirder than that actually - if&nbsp;
we take all these integrals and include yet&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:00.840 --> 00:04:06.060
another factor 2cos(x) which again, you would&nbsp;
think changes their values entirely - you can't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:06.060 --> 00:04:10.560
just randomly multiply new things into an&nbsp;
this, it continues to equal pi for much&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:10.560 --> 00:04:16.080
much longer and it's not until you get to the&nbsp;
number 113 that it breaks. And when it breaks,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:16.080 --> 00:04:19.620
it's by the most puny absolutely&nbsp;
subtle amount that you could imagine.

00:04:20.340 --> 00:04:25.080
So, the natural question is - what on&nbsp;
earth is going on here? And luckily&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:25.080 --> 00:04:28.740
there actually is a really satisfying&nbsp;
explanation for all this. The way I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:28.740 --> 00:04:32.160
think I'll go about this is to show you&nbsp;
a phenomenon that first looks completely&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:32.160 --> 00:04:36.420
unrelated but it shows a similar pattern&nbsp;
where you have a value that stays really&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:36.420 --> 00:04:40.680
stable until you get to number 15 and&nbsp;
then it falters by just a tiny amount.

00:04:40.680 --> 00:04:44.100
And then after that, I'll show&nbsp;
why this seemingly unrelated&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:44.100 --> 00:04:49.260
phenomenon is secretly the same as all our&nbsp;
integral expressions but in disguise. So,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:49.260 --> 00:04:53.280
turning our attention to what seems completely&nbsp;
different - consider a function that I'm going&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:53.280 --> 00:04:59.040
to be calling rect(x) which is defined&nbsp;
to equal 1 if the input is between -1/2&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:59.580 --> 00:05:04.680
and 1/2 and otherwise it's equal to 0. So,&nbsp;
the function is this boring step basically.

00:05:04.680 --> 00:05:10.260
This will be the first in a sequence of function&nbsp;
that we define, so I'll call it f1(x) and each&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:10.260 --> 00:05:14.700
new function in our sequence is going to be a&nbsp;
kind of moving average of the previous function.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:15.660 --> 00:05:20.280
So, for example, the way the second iteration will&nbsp;
be defined is to take this sliding window whose&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:20.280 --> 00:05:26.820
width is 1/3 and for a particular input X, when&nbsp;
the window is centred at that input X, the value&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:26.820 --> 00:05:32.280
in my new function drawn below is defined to be&nbsp;
equal to the average value of the first function&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:32.280 --> 00:05:36.960
above inside that window. So, for example when&nbsp;
the window is far enough to the left, every value&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:36.960 --> 00:05:41.880
inside it is zero, so the graph on the bottom is&nbsp;
showing zero. As soon as that window starts to go&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:41.880 --> 00:05:46.440
over the plateau a little bit, the average value&nbsp;
is a little more than zero and you see that in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:46.440 --> 00:05:51.120
graph below. And notice that when exactly half&nbsp;
the window is over that plateau at one and half&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:51.120 --> 00:05:56.220
of it is at zero, the corresponding value in the&nbsp;
bottom graph is one half and you get the point.

00:05:56.220 --> 00:06:01.740
The important thing I want you to focus on is how&nbsp;
when that window is entirely in the plateau above&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:01.740 --> 00:06:06.900
where all the values are one, then the average&nbsp;
value is also one, so we get this plateau on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:06.900 --> 00:06:11.760
our function at the bottom. Let's call this&nbsp;
bottom function f2(x) and what I want you to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:11.760 --> 00:06:16.680
think about is the length of the plateau for&nbsp;
that second function. How wide should it be?

00:06:16.680 --> 00:06:22.140
If you think about it for a moment, the distance&nbsp;
between the left edge of the top plateau and the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:22.140 --> 00:06:26.940
left edge of the bottom plateau will be exactly&nbsp;
half of the width of the window. So, half of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:26.940 --> 00:06:32.100
one third and similarly on the right side, the&nbsp;
distance between the edges of the plateaus is half&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:32.100 --> 00:06:36.780
of the window width. So, overall, it's one minus&nbsp;
that window width, which is one minus a third.

00:06:37.380 --> 00:06:40.800
The value we're going to be computing,&nbsp;
the thing that will look stable for a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:40.800 --> 00:06:45.660
while before it breaks is the value of this&nbsp;
function at the input zero, which in both of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:45.660 --> 00:06:50.280
these iterations is equal to one because it's&nbsp;
inside that plateau. For the next iteration,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:50.280 --> 00:06:54.060
we're going to take a moving average of that&nbsp;
last function but this time with the window&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:54.060 --> 00:06:58.740
whose width is one fifth. It's kind of fun to&nbsp;
think about why as you slide around this window,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:58.740 --> 00:07:03.540
you get a smooth out version of the previous&nbsp;
function and again, the significant thing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:03.540 --> 00:07:07.800
I want you to focus on is how when that&nbsp;
window is entirely inside the plateau of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:07.800 --> 00:07:11.760
the previous function, then, by definition,&nbsp;
the bottom function is going to equal one.

00:07:11.760 --> 00:07:16.260
This time, the length of that plateau on the&nbsp;
bottom will be the length of the previous one,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:16.260 --> 00:07:21.960
1-1/3 minus the window width, 1/5th. The&nbsp;
reasoning is the same as before - in order&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:21.960 --> 00:07:25.740
to go from the point where the middle of&nbsp;
the window is on that top plateau to where&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:25.740 --> 00:07:29.340
the entirety of the window is inside&nbsp;
that plateau is half the window width&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:29.340 --> 00:07:33.600
and likewise on the right side and once&nbsp;
more, the value to record is the output&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:33.600 --> 00:07:37.200
of this function when the input is&nbsp;
zero. Which again, is exactly one.

00:07:38.460 --> 00:07:43.140
The next iteration is a moving average with the&nbsp;
window width of 1/7th - the plateau gets smaller&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:43.140 --> 00:07:49.020
by that 1/7. Doing one more iteration with 1/9,&nbsp;
the plateau gets smaller by amount. And as we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:49.020 --> 00:07:54.600
keep going, the plateau gets thinner and thinner&nbsp;
and also notice how just outside of the plateau,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:54.600 --> 00:07:59.040
the function is really really close to one because&nbsp;
it's always been the result of an average between&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:59.040 --> 00:08:02.700
the plateau at one and the neighbours, which&nbsp;
themselves are really really close to one.

00:08:03.300 --> 00:08:06.960
The point at which all of this breaks&nbsp;
is once we get to the iteration where&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:06.960 --> 00:08:10.260
we're sliding a window with width&nbsp;
1/15th across the whole thing.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:10.920 --> 00:08:15.300
At that point, the previous plateau is&nbsp;
actually thinner than the window itself. So,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:15.300 --> 00:08:21.480
even at the input X=0, this moving average have&nbsp;
to be ever so slightly smaller than one. And the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:21.480 --> 00:08:26.100
only thing that's special about the number 15 here&nbsp;
is that as we keep adding the reciprocals of these&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:26.100 --> 00:08:31.320
odd fractions - 1/3 plus 1/5th plus 1/7th,&nbsp;
on and on, it's once we get to 1/15th that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:31.320 --> 00:08:36.600
that sum grows to be bigger than one and in the&nbsp;
context of our shrinking plateaus, having started&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:36.600 --> 00:08:41.700
with a plateau of width one, it's now shrunk&nbsp;
down so much that it will disappear entirely.

00:08:41.700 --> 00:08:46.860
The point is, with this as a sequence of functions&nbsp;
that we've defined by seemingly random procedure,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:46.860 --> 00:08:50.520
if I ask you to compute the values of&nbsp;
all of these functions at the input zero,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:50.520 --> 00:08:55.920
you get a pattern which initially looks stable,&nbsp;
it's one, one, one, one, one, one, one but by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:55.920 --> 00:09:00.180
the time we get to the eighth iteration, it&nbsp;
falls short ever so slightly, just barely.

00:09:00.900 --> 00:09:05.400
This is analogous, and I claim more than just&nbsp;
analogous to the integrals we saw earlier,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:05.400 --> 00:09:10.560
where you have a stable value at pi, pi, pi,&nbsp;
pi, pi until it falls short just barely. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:10.560 --> 00:09:15.600
as it happens, this constant from our moving&nbsp;
average process that's ever so slightly smaller&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:15.600 --> 00:09:20.640
than one is exactly the factor that sits in&nbsp;
front of pi in our series of integrals. So,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:20.640 --> 00:09:24.900
the two situations aren't just qualitatively&nbsp;
similar, they're quantitatively the same as well.

00:09:24.900 --> 00:09:29.880
And when it comes to the case where we add&nbsp;
the 2 cosine of X term inside the integral,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:29.880 --> 00:09:34.080
which cause the pattern to last a lot&nbsp;
longer before it broke down, in the analogy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:34.080 --> 00:09:37.620
what that will correspond to is the same&nbsp;
setup but where the function we start with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:37.620 --> 00:09:43.740
has an even longer plateau - stretching from&nbsp;
x = -1 up to 1, meaning its length is 2. So,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:43.740 --> 00:09:48.060
as you do this repeated moving average process,&nbsp;
eating into it with smaller and smaller windows,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:48.060 --> 00:09:53.040
it takes a lot longer for them to eat into the&nbsp;
whole plateau. More specifically, the relevant&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:53.040 --> 00:09:58.200
computation is to ask - how long do you have to&nbsp;
add these reciprocals of odd numbers until that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:58.200 --> 00:10:03.900
sum becomes bigger than two? And it turns out that&nbsp;
you have to go until you hit the number 113. Which&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:03.900 --> 00:10:08.700
will correspond to the fact that the integral&nbsp;
pattern there continues until you hit 113.

00:10:09.240 --> 00:10:13.320
And by the way, I should emphasize that there&nbsp;
is nothing special about these reciprocals of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:13.320 --> 00:10:17.760
odd numbers; one third, one fifth, one seventh&nbsp;
- that just Happens to be the sequence of values&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:17.760 --> 00:10:21.660
highlighted by the Borweins in their paper&nbsp;
that made the sequence mildly famous in nerd&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:21.660 --> 00:10:26.520
circles. More generally, we could be inserting&nbsp;
any sequence of positive numbers into those sinc&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:26.520 --> 00:10:31.440
functions and as long as the sum of those numbers&nbsp;
is less than one, our expression will equal pi,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:31.440 --> 00:10:36.300
but as soon as they become bigger than one, our&nbsp;
expression drops a little below pi and if you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:36.300 --> 00:10:39.840
believe me that there's an analogy with these&nbsp;
moving averages, you can hopefully see why.

00:10:42.060 --> 00:10:46.740
But of course, the burning question is why&nbsp;
on earth should these two situations have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:46.740 --> 00:10:51.540
anything to do with each other? From here,&nbsp;
the argument does bring in two mildly heavy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:51.540 --> 00:10:57.060
bits of machinery. Namely Fourier Transforms&nbsp;
and convolutions. And the way I'd like to go&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:57.060 --> 00:11:00.960
about this is to spend the remainder of this&nbsp;
video giving you a high level sense of how&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:00.960 --> 00:11:04.620
the argument will go without necessarily&nbsp;
assuming you're familiar with either of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:04.620 --> 00:11:08.880
those two topics and then to explain why the&nbsp;
details are true in a video that's dedicated&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:08.880 --> 00:11:13.380
to convolutions. In particular something&nbsp;
called The Convolution Theorem since it's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:13.380 --> 00:11:18.120
incredibly beautiful and it's useful well&nbsp;
beyond this specific very esoteric question.

00:11:20.640 --> 00:11:26.820
To start, instead of focusing on this function&nbsp;
sin(x)/x, where we want to show why the signed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:26.820 --> 00:11:31.020
area underneath its curve is equal to pi,&nbsp;
we'll make a simple substitution where we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:31.020 --> 00:11:36.180
replace the input X with π*x, which has the&nbsp;
effect of squishing the graph horizontally&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:36.180 --> 00:11:40.380
by a factor of pi and so, the area&nbsp;
gets scaled down by a factor of pi,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:40.380 --> 00:11:44.940
meaning our new goal is to show why this&nbsp;
integral on the right is equal to exactly one.

00:11:44.940 --> 00:11:50.520
By the way, in some engineering context, people&nbsp;
use the name "sinc" to refer to this function with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:50.520 --> 00:11:55.080
the pi on the inside since it's often very nice&nbsp;
to have a normalized function, meaning the area&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:55.080 --> 00:11:59.340
under it is equal to one. The point is, showing&nbsp;
this integral on the right is exactly the same&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:59.340 --> 00:12:03.480
thing as showing the integral on the left, it's&nbsp;
just a change of variables. And likewise for all&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:03.480 --> 00:12:09.600
of the other ones in our sequence, go through each&nbsp;
of them, replace the X with the π*x and from here,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:09.600 --> 00:12:13.680
the claim is that all these integrals are not&nbsp;
