00:13:32.880 --> 00:13:37.860
something upon making this translation. And&nbsp;
the real idea behind why Fourier Transforms&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:37.860 --> 00:13:41.280
are such a useful thing for math is that&nbsp;
when you take statements and questions&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:41.280 --> 00:13:46.320
about a particular function and then you look&nbsp;
at what they correspond to with respect to the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:46.320 --> 00:13:50.340
transformed version of that function, those&nbsp;
statements and questions often look very very&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:50.340 --> 00:13:55.020
different in this new language, and sometimes&nbsp;
it makes the questions a lot easier to answer.

00:13:55.020 --> 00:13:59.280
For example, one very nice little fact,&nbsp;
another thing on our list of things to show,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:59.280 --> 00:14:03.660
is that if you want to compute the integral of&nbsp;
some function from negative infinity to infinity,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:03.660 --> 00:14:08.760
this signed area under the entirety of&nbsp;
its curve, it's the same thing as simply&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:08.760 --> 00:14:14.700
evaluating the Fourier Transformed version&nbsp;
of that function at the input zero. This is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:14.700 --> 00:14:19.020
a fact that will actually just pop right out&nbsp;
of the definition and representative of a more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:19.020 --> 00:14:23.700
general vibe that every individual output of&nbsp;
the Fourier Transform function on the right&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:23.700 --> 00:14:28.260
corresponds to some kind of global information&nbsp;
about the original function on the left.

00:14:28.260 --> 00:14:32.880
In our specific case, it means if you&nbsp;
believe me that this sinc function and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:32.880 --> 00:14:35.700
the rect function are related with&nbsp;
the Fourier Transform like this,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:35.700 --> 00:14:39.900
it explains the integral which is otherwise&nbsp;
a very tricky thing to compute because it's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:39.900 --> 00:14:45.120
saying all that signed area is the same thing&nbsp;
as evaluating rect at zero which is just one.

00:14:46.200 --> 00:14:50.520
Now, you could complain - surely this just moves&nbsp;
the bump under the rug. Surely computing this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:50.520 --> 00:14:54.240
Fourier Transform, whatever that looks like&nbsp;
would be as hard as computing the original&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:54.240 --> 00:14:58.140
integral. But the idea is that there's lots&nbsp;
of tips and tricks for computing these Fourier&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:58.140 --> 00:15:02.940
Transforms and moreover that when you do,&nbsp;
it tells you a lot more information than&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:02.940 --> 00:15:06.420
just that integral. You get a lot of bang&nbsp;
for your buck out of doing the computation.

00:15:06.420 --> 00:15:11.040
Now, the other key fact that will explain&nbsp;
the connection we're hunting for is that if&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:11.040 --> 00:15:15.180
you have two different functions and you&nbsp;
take their product and then you take the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:15.180 --> 00:15:19.920
Fourier Transform of that product, it would be&nbsp;
the same thing as if you individually took the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:19.920 --> 00:15:24.120
Fourier Transforms of your original function&nbsp;
and then combined them using a new kind of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:24.120 --> 00:15:27.780
operation that we'll talk all about in&nbsp;
the next video known as a Convolution.

00:15:27.780 --> 00:15:32.640
Now, even though there's a lot to be explained&nbsp;
with convolutions, the upshot will be that in our&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:32.640 --> 00:15:38.580
specific case with these rectangular functions,&nbsp;
taking a convolution looks just like one of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:38.580 --> 00:15:42.360
moving averages that we've been talking about&nbsp;
this whole time. Combined with our previous&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:42.360 --> 00:15:47.280
fact that integrating in one context looks&nbsp;
like evaluating at zero in another context,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:47.280 --> 00:15:52.680
if you believe me, that multiplying in one&nbsp;
context corresponds to this new operation,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:52.680 --> 00:15:55.920
convolutions, which for our example you&nbsp;
should just think of as moving averages,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:55.920 --> 00:16:00.600
that will explain why multiplying more and&nbsp;
more of these sinc functions together can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:00.600 --> 00:16:04.920
be thought about in terms of these progressive&nbsp;
moving averages and always evaluating at zero,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:04.920 --> 00:16:09.780
which in turn gives a really lovely intuition for&nbsp;
why you would expect such a stable value before&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:09.780 --> 00:16:14.100
eventually something breaks down as the edges of&nbsp;
the plateau inch closer and closer to the center.

00:16:15.480 --> 00:16:19.980
This last key fact by the way has a special name,&nbsp;
it's called the Convolution Theorem and again,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:19.980 --> 00:16:21.960
it's something that we'll&nbsp;
go into much more deeply.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:22.800 --> 00:16:27.960
I recognize that it's maybe a little unsatisfying&nbsp;
to end things here by laying down three magical&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:27.960 --> 00:16:31.920
facts and saying "everything follows from&nbsp;
those", but hopefully this gives you a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:31.920 --> 00:16:37.200
little glimpse of why powerful tools like Fourier&nbsp;
Transforms can be so useful for tricky problems.

00:16:37.740 --> 00:16:42.600
It's a systematic way to provide a shift&nbsp;
in perspective where hard problems can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:42.600 --> 00:16:46.500
sometimes look easier. If nothing else, it&nbsp;
hopefully provides some motivation to learn&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:46.500 --> 00:16:49.080
about these beautiful things&nbsp;
like the convolution theorem.

00:16:49.080 --> 00:16:53.640
As one more tiny teaser, another fun&nbsp;
consequence of this convolution theorem&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:53.640 --> 00:16:57.840
will be that it opens the doors for an algorithm&nbsp;
that lets you compute the product of two large&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:57.840 --> 00:17:01.980
numbers very quickly - like way faster&nbsp;
than you think should be even possible.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:02.940 --> 00:17:04.740
So with that, I'll see you in the next video.

